      First, NetHack with X11 interface is not xnethack.  Calling it that
will just lead to confusion.  (There used to be some hacked up version of
xterm for running NetHack with some extra graphical bells and whistles
added, which some people called "xnethack".  That was a bit confusing too,
but it'd be doubly so now.)  NetHack with the X interface is the same as
NetHack with the terminal interface, not a traditional event driven X
application.

     NetHack's X interface does not accept any options from the command
line, other than the handful accepted by the regular version.  It does not
even accept the the choice of X11 vs tty for programs compiled to work
with both.  [That will probably change some day.]

     In the sources, there's an "application defaults" (win/X11/nethack.ad
or something close to that) file which should be customized and put into
place.  It depends on your window manager whether "put into place" means
added into "~/.Xdefaults" or copied as "nethack" to some particular 
directory or possibly some other variant.  You'll probably want to take
win/X11/nethack.rc and put in into place as "~/.nethackrc" too.  This
stuff should be covered in win/X11/Install.X11 which you need to follow
in addition to sys/unix/Install.unx when building for X Windows under Unix.



